Presidential elections were held in [[Austria]] on 24 April 2016, with a [[Two-round system|second round]] run-off on 22 May 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/2000028904548/Termin-fuer-Wahl-des-Bundespraesidenten-naechste-Woche-fix |title=Wahltermin wird nächste Woche fixiert – Bundespräsident – derStandard.at › Inland |website=Derstandard.at |access-date=5 April 2016}}</ref> However, the results of the second round were annulled and a re-vote took place on 4 December 2016.<ref name=":0" /><ref name="postpone">Klatzer, Jürgen, Daniela Wahl and Peter Temel, [https://kurier.at/politik/inland/hofburg-wahl-entscheidung-ueber-wahlverschiebung/220.731.631 "Hofburg-Wahl: Stichwahl auf 4.12. verschoben, Jungwähler kommen in Wählerregister" (in German)], ''kurier.at'', 12 September 2016.</ref>

Incumbent President [[Heinz Fischer]] had served two terms and was not eligible to be elected for a third successive term. In the first round of the election, [[Norbert Hofer]] of the [[Freedom Party of Austria]] (FPÖ) received the most votes. [[Alexander Van der Bellen]], an independent candidate who formerly a member of both the [[The Greens – The Green Alternative|Austrian Greens]] and the [[Social Democratic Party of Austria|Social Democratic]], placed second. The candidates of the two governing parties, the [[Social Democratic Party of Austria|Social Democratic]] and [[Austrian People's Party|Austrian People's]] parties, placed fourth and fifth respectively, behind independent [[Irmgard Griss]] in third place, which led to a government crisis and the resignation of Chancellor [[Werner Faymann]]. Since no candidate received a majority of the vote, Hofer and Van der Bellen went head-to-head in the second round in May. This was the first time since the [[Second World War]] that an Austrian president had not been backed by either the People's or the Social Democratic party.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/apr/24/austrian-far-right-wins-first-round-presidential-election-norbert-hofer|title=Austrian far-right party wins first round of presidential election|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]|date=25 April 2016|access-date=25 April 2016}}</ref> During the run-off, Van der Bellen defeated Hofer on 23 May 2016 after the postal ballots had been counted.<ref>{{cite news|title=Austria far-right 'narrowly loses poll, electing Van der Bellen president'|work=BBC News |date=23 May 2016 |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-36362505|access-date=23 May 2016}}</ref>

On 1 July, the results of the second round of voting were annulled after the results in 20 of the 117 administrative districts were challenged, and the [[Constitutional Court of Austria]] found that Austrian electoral law had been disregarded in 14 of them. The Court found that over 77,900 absentee votes were improperly counted too early, though no indication of fraud was found.<ref name=over>{{cite news|last1=Oltermann|first1=Philip|title=Austrian presidential election result overturned and must be held again|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/jul/01/austrian-presidential-election-result-overturned-and-must-be-held-again-hofer-van-der-bellen|access-date=1 July 2016|work=The Guardian|date=1 July 2016}}</ref> The second round re-vote was planned on 2 October, but was postponed to 4 December 2016.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/sep/12/austria-presidential-election-rerun-to-be-postponed-faulty-glue-ballot-papers|title=Austrian presidential election to be postponed due to faulty glue|website=[[TheGuardian.com]] |language=en-GB|access-date=5 July 2016}}</ref><ref name="postpone"/>

Van der Bellen ultimately won the second round re-vote with 53.8% of the vote and a voter turnout of 74.2%.<ref name=":1">''[http://wahl16.bmi.gv.at/ Österreich, vorläufiges Endergebnis inklusive Briefwahlstimmen.] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160523155755/http://wahl16.bmi.gv.at/ |date=23 May 2016 }} ''Bundesministerium für'' Inneres'' (in German). Retrieved 10 December 2016.</ref> Hofer conceded the race to Van der Bellen when the result had become apparent shortly after polls closed.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/dec/04/far-right-party-concedes-defeat-in-austrian-presidential-election|title=Far-right candidate concedes defeat in Austrian election|last=Oltermann|first=Philip|date=4 December 2016|newspaper=The Guardian|language=en-GB|issn=0261-3077|access-date=4 December 2016}}</ref> Van der Bellen was sworn in as the twelfth president of Austria on 26 January 2017.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.dw.com/en/van-der-bellen-takes-office-as-austrian-president/a-37282299|title=Van der Bellen takes office as Austrian president|newspaper=Deutsche Wells|access-date=7 Feb 2017}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The [[president of Austria]] is directly elected by universal adult suffrage once every six years. The election is held under a [[two-round system]]; if no candidate receives more than 50% of votes cast in the first round, then a second ballot occurs in which only those two candidates who received the greatest number of votes in the first round may stand. The [[Constitution of Austria|constitution]] grants the president the power to appoint the [[Chancellor of Austria|chancellor]] and, by extension, federal cabinet ministers, Supreme Court justices, military officers, and most major bureaucrats. The president may dissolve the [[National Council (Austria)|National Council]]. In practice, however, the president acts as a [[figurehead]].

==Candidates==
===Social Democratic Party (SPÖ)===
The most likely candidate of the [[Social Democratic Party of Austria|Social Democratic Party]] was considered to be Labour Minister [[Rudolf Hundstorfer]], though President of the National Council [[Doris Bures]], former Chancellor [[Franz Vranitzky]] and former undersecretary for EU affairs {{Interlanguage link multi|Brigitte Ederer|de}} were also mentioned.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/Duell-um-die-Hofburg/218771863 |title=Duell um die Hofburg |website=Oe24.at |date=6 January 2016 |access-date=5 April 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://kurier.at/politik/inland/das-jahr-2016-bringt-neues-spitzenpersonal/172.518.058 |title=Das Jahr 2016 bringt neues Spitzenpersonal |website=Kurier.At |date=1 January 2016 |access-date=5 April 2016}}</ref> On 15 January 2016, Hundstorfer was officially announced as the SPÖ's candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/2000029105205/SPOe-gibt-Praesidentschaftskandidaten-bekannt |title=SPÖ-Kandidat Hundstorfer betont nötigen Zusammenhalt in Flüchtlingsfrage – Bundespräsident – derStandard.at › Inland |website=Derstandard.at |access-date=5 April 2016}}</ref>

