==Result cancelled==
On 8 June, FPÖ chairman [[Heinz-Christian Strache]] brought a 152-page appeal to the [[Constitutional Court (Austria)|Constitutional Court]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://wirtschaftsblatt.at/home/nachrichten/oesterreich/5005805/FPOWahlanfechtung-eingebracht-HC-Strache_Hofer-haette-Praesident|title=FPÖ-Wahlanfechtung eingebracht – HC Strache: Hofer hätte Präsident werden können|work=wirtschaftsblatt.at}}</ref> Strache claimed that more than 30,000 votes had been prematurely tallied, more than 50,000 votes had been counted by unauthorized personnel. Also, there was a pre-sorting of absentee ballots (500,000 of them) into two batches - void ballots and countable ballots (this is permissible as long as both batches are then re-checked by the commission).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://kurier.at/politik/inland/faktencheck-wie-lauten-die-fpoe-vorwuerfe-und-was-ist-dran/203.584.185|title=Faktencheck: Wie lauten die FPÖ-Vorwürfe und was ist dran?|work=kurier.at|date=9 June 2016 }}</ref> Other charges included minors and non-citizens having been allowed to vote.<ref name="bbc.com">{{cite news|title=Austria presidential poll result overturned|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-36681475|date=1 July 2016|access-date=9 December 2016}}</ref> The [[Austrian Interior Ministry]] acknowledged some irregularities but said that the number of votes affected was not enough to overturn the results. "There was sloppiness", said Interior Minister Wolfgang Sobotka.<ref name="reuters">{{cite web|url=https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-austria-election/austrian-far-right-gets-second-chance-at-presidency-with-vote-re-run-idUKKCN0ZH4K3|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181127191424/https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-austria-election/austrian-far-right-gets-second-chance-at-presidency-with-vote-re-run-idUKKCN0ZH4K3|url-status=dead|archive-date=27 November 2018|title=Austrian far right gets second chance at presidency with vote re-run|access-date=1 July 2016|work=Reuters}}</ref>

Counsel for Van der Bellen argued that the irregularities would have had only an "insignificant" impact on results, while lawyers for the FPÖ said they could have affected the results of the election.<ref name = "irishtimes.com">{{cite news|url=http://www.irishtimes.com/news/world/europe/austrian-presidential-election-must-be-held-again-court-rules-1.2706682|title=Austrian presidential election must be held again, court rules|newspaper=[[The Irish Times]]|access-date=1 July 2016}}</ref> But on 1 July, since Hofer had lost to Van der Bellen by 30,863 votes and the Court found that more than twice that number (77,926<ref name="irishtimes.com"/>) had been affected by breaches of the electoral code,<ref name="reuters"/> The Constitutional Court ordered that the second round be held again.<ref name="bbc.com"/> Noting the irregularities, the Court said in a statement: "It is completely clear to the Constitutional Court that laws regulating an election must be rigorously applied ... This must rule out abuse and manipulations."<ref name="WSJRerun">{{cite web|last=Troianovski|first=Anton|author-link=Anton Troianovski|date=1 July 2016|title=Austrian Court Orders Rerun of Presidential Vote|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/austrian-court-orders-rerun-of-presidential-vote-1467369788|url-access=subscription|website=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|location=Berlin}}</ref>

Before the Court's ruling, Van der Bellen was scheduled to be sworn in as president on 9 July. Elections were set for 2 October 2016.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-36714480|title=Austria to re-run overturned presidential poll in October – BBC News|work=BBC News |date=5 July 2016 }}</ref> Until then, outgoing Austrian president [[Heinz Fischer]] would be replaced on an interim basis by the three presidents of the [[National Council (Austria)|National Council]], a "National President Council", of which Hofer is the third member.<ref name="irishtimes.com"/> Van der Bellen said that Austrians were "shocked and unsettled" by the Court's ruling, but that he expected to win a second time.<ref name="WSJRerun"/>

On 12 September the Federal Minister of the Interior, Wolfgang Sobotka, announced that due to faulty glue in the voting envelopes, the rerun of the second round was postponed until 4 December 2016.<ref name="postpone"/><ref name="BBC postpone">{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-37336947|title=Austria presidential election: Faulty envelope glue delays re-run|date=12 September 2016|publisher=BBC}}</ref> He also said that Austrian law allowed the election to be postponed only in case of death of a candidate, therefore the decision had to be made by parliament.<ref>{{cite web|title=Repeat of Austria's Presidential Runoff Faces a Delay (see video)|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/13/world/europe/austria-president-election.html|website=The New York Times|date=12 September 2016}}</ref> The delay also allowed for some young Austrians who turned 16 after May to vote in the rerun.<ref name="BBC postpone"/> FPÖ Chairman Heinz-Christian Strache criticized postponing the elections, claiming it was done by political opponents because Hofer was doing well in the polls.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-37336947|title=Austria presidential election: Faulty envelope glue delays re-run|first=Kate|last=Connolly|date=12 September 2016|work=The Guardian}}</ref>

